  "dom_id": "step-20636",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "A common problem in statistics is recognizing whether data follows a trend. Thankfully, we can often identify trends in data simply by looking at a scatter plot. We list a few common examples below:\n\n- **Positive linear correlation**: The points seem to follow a straight line with a positive slope.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226611/assets/e5b155a661e5cb548c30cc34af0933087a4b63b63295b4667757c355d1a32802.png)\n\n- **Negative linear correlation**: The points seem to follow a straight line with a negative slope.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226611/assets/3dc0506d5686bbc583a86960343a6d4019071edb45121a9688d4b482f16ac8f8.png)\n\n- **Quadratic correlation**: The points on the plot are scattered along a parabola.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226611/assets/53ed6cecb371e0f31623803568d6e1d97057652f13156da9cc71a64f91beadb3.png)\n\n- **Exponential correlation**: The points on the plot are scattered along an exponential curve.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226611/assets/2976d466ec40ff595f15f78b0a90e0f0ecc34c8c43c4f10179b7f124f73330f9.png)\n\n- **No correlation**: The points seem to be scattered randomly.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226611/assets/eb025d0075bf8c8f6ac236759fa786b3c7f145a8187e8c219f7fe0ee82d3a0f7.png)\n\nThe process of fitting data to a linear, quadratic, exponential, or another type of curve is called **regression**.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
